**Without DI**:
```java
public class UserService {
    private UserRepository repo = new UserRepository(); // creates its own dep
}
```

**With DI**:
```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserService(UserRepository repo) { // receives dep from outside
        this.repo = repo;
    }
}
```
Spring calls `new UserService(userRepositoryInstance)` automatically, injecting the repository.

```java
public interface NotificationService { void send(String msg); }

@Service
@Primary  // default when no qualifier specified
public class EmailNotificationService implements NotificationService { ... }

@Service
@Qualifier("sms")
public class SmsNotificationService implements NotificationService { ... }

@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Autowired
    private NotificationService service; // gets EmailNotificationService (@Primary)

    @Autowired @Qualifier("sms")
    private NotificationService smsService; // explicitly gets SmsNotificationService
}
```

The **Dependency Inversion Principle** states: *high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules; both should depend on abstractions*.

In Spring:
```java
// High-level module depends on ABSTRACTION (interface)
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final PaymentService paymentService; // interface, not impl
    // Spring injects StripePaymentService at runtime
}
```
`OrderService` (high-level) doesn't depend on `StripePaymentService` (low-level) directly — it depends on the `PaymentService` interface. Spring bridges them.
